Author: minh

--- log ---
2026-08-21T13:48Z minh created

2026-08-21T14:08Z decisions reviewed with author: schema revised to FTS5 + indexed side table (362ms vs 0ms measured), query sanitisation named, makeSnippet retained
--- body ---
## Context

The first child, and the only one carrying design risk: everything after it
depends on the schema and on how `SqliteSearchIndex` relates to the existing
class. Tracker: 0001.

## Decisions taken (reviewed 2026-08-21)

**Schema — a plain FTS5 table plus an indexed side table.** Not external
content, and not FTS5 alone. An `UNINDEXED` FTS5 column is not indexed, so a
per-item lookup is a full scan: measured on the 408 628-passage bench,
**362 ms by item against 0 ms for an indexed MATCH**. Ticket 0006 deletes by
item on every delta, so a fifty-item delta would cost eighteen seconds.

    CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE passages USING fts5(
      body, tokenize='unicode61 remove_diacritics 2');
    CREATE TABLE passage_meta(
      rowid INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,   -- = passages.rowid
      item TEXT NOT NULL, ord INTEGER NOT NULL,
      title TEXT, source TEXT      -- NULL for metadata, 'fulltext' otherwise
    );
    CREATE INDEX passage_meta_item ON passage_meta(item);

FTS5 carries only the text; everything else sits in an ordinary table with a
real index on `item`. That side table is also where 0004's vector BLOB goes.

**Tokenizer** `unicode61 remove_diacritics 2`, verified to fold accents. No
stemming: FTS5's `porter` is English-only and the corpus is not. Do **not**
add `prefix='2 3'` here — it would make prefix queries cheap, and prefix is the
only morphology available in French, but zoteus issues no prefix queries today,
so enabling it changes behaviour nothing asked to change.

**Query sanitisation is a named piece of work, not a detail.** `MATCH` takes a
query *language*: `"`, `*`, `NEAR`, `OR`, `-`, `(` are syntax there. zoteus's
`BM25Index.search(q)` receives the raw string and tokenises it itself, so any
input is safe today. Passing `args.q` to `MATCH` would turn a user query
containing an apostrophe into a SQLite syntax error surfaced as a tool failure.
Tokenise the query with the same tokenizer as the index, quote each term, join
with `OR` — zoteus's BM25 ORs its terms, while FTS5 defaults to an implicit AND,
so this also settles the semantics.

**Keep `makeSnippet`, do not adopt FTS5's `snippet()`.** `query()` calls
`makeSnippet(rec.text, q)` and so needs the passage text at query time; return
`body` from the row. FTS5's `snippet()` is better — it knows token offsets
rather than searching for a substring — and that is exactly why it is out of
scope here: it would change the output and break the same-results-out
comparison that is this chantier's load-bearing gate. Switching to it later is
a deliberate, separate change.

**File location** `<ZOTEUS_DATA_DIR>/search-index.sqlite`, beside
`search-index.json`, so both coexist during 0005.

**Shadow, do not replace.** `SqliteSearchIndex` is a second implementation
selected by config, defaulting to the existing path. Without both live at once
there is nothing to compare, and the comparison is the gate.

## Relevant files

- `fork/src/features/search/index-manager.ts` — the `SearchIndex` interface to satisfy
- `fork/src/features/search/bm25.ts`, `tokenize.ts` — what FTS5 replaces
- `bench/fts5_bench.mjs` — the standalone prototype the schema comes from

## Test

Red step: run upstream's existing search tests against `SqliteSearchIndex`
instead of `SearchIndex`. They fail on the unimplemented methods first, then
pass as each lands. No new test fixtures — reusing upstream's is the point.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `SqliteSearchIndex` satisfies the `SearchIndex` interface, keyword-only
- [ ] Upstream's suite passes against it
- [ ] Query semantics match the current BM25 on a shared sample of queries
- [ ] `tsc --noEmit` and `eslint` clean
